playable.evidence:
Limits as stated: 16 steps per round (<=20), 4 choice steps (<=4), 16 attributes (<=20), 2 hidden elements (hands and the aside pile, <=3), 36 rounds (<=40). The single shuffle per hand is 1 random draw in a round (<=1). Turns are sequential by design, which is legitimate because order is the point of a climbing game. No talk with 3 players is sensible: there is nothing to negotiate that the play itself does not express. There is no board, clock or judge, and the winner is pure arithmetic.

Problem 1, a contradiction in the round structure. 'Each hand lasts up to 12 rounds, so the game has 36 rounds in total' contradicts 'A hand ends at the end of the first round in which at least one player has no cards left ... The next hand is then dealt.' Suppose hand 1 ends early in round 8. Either hand 2 starts in round 9, in which case the game ends after hand 3 before round 36 and the engine's fixed 36 rounds have undefined rounds left over. Or hand 2 waits until round 13, in which case rounds 9-12 have no defined content. The rules do not say which. The rule 'leader of the first trick is seat 2 in hand 2' also cannot be placed in a fixed round without this answer.

Problem 2, the step budget is not shown and looks over 20. Per round the engine needs:
- 4 choice steps;
- for each play: a legality check (shape, value above the current play, triple beats a single or pair), card removal, updating the current play's value, shape and owner, and a +3 bomb update;
- next-lead assignment (winner, or the seat after the bomber);
- an empty-hand check;
- conditional at hand start: a 40-card shuffle and dealing 36 cards plus 4 aside (shared_deck_draw is a workaround costing remove/append steps);
- conditional at hand end: 3 card-count penalties, 3 ten-surcharges and 3 going-out bonuses, plus setting the hand's first leader.
Written as a flat list of single steps with `when` conditions, this plausibly exceeds 20. The stated 16 is unsupported.

Problem 3, minor ambiguities. It is unclear whether a player holding a triple (not four of a kind) may split it into a single or a pair. The rule mentions only four of a kind. It is also unclear whether hand sizes are public.

reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
Chance enters through the deal: the number of 10s, the triples and the pair structure. The 4 aside cards add uncertainty to counting. A hand with two triples and two 10s is clearly stronger than one with no pairs. Three independent deals average this out somewhat, and the lead rotation is fair.

Skill decisions remain substantial:
- counting the public discard pile (36 of 40 cards end up in hands, and every play and pass is public), to infer who can still beat a 10 or a pair;
- choosing between single and pair leads to control the tempo under a 12-round clock;
- using the turn-4 last word;
- deciding when a net-zero bomb is worth giving the lead to the next seat, which means modelling who sits after you;
- the tension that 10s are both the best control cards and the costliest to hold (2 each);
- timing going out for the -3 bonus.

Estimate: deal luck accounts for roughly 35-45% of single-hand outcomes and somewhat less over three hands.

When all three play well, the outcome is not decided by the deal alone. Penalties are close (each typically 0-8 per hand), and lead control, bomb timing and counting errors swing several points. A weak deal can be partly rescued by holding back control cards and denying leads. A reasoning player is clearly favoured over a thoughtless one (policies a-c above lose by tens of points). There is real hidden information to infer (opponents' hands and the 4 unseen cards) and a concrete way to act on it (when to pass, beat or bomb).
